653: \section{Operational interface}
654: 
655: \paragraph{Reader map.}
656: The supplement separates one central theorem chain from support-boundary audit
657: records.  The core finite-record safety argument is:
658: Definition~\ref{def:operational-acceptance-interface} fixes the certificate tuple,
659: claim kinds, gate dispatch, verifier predicate, evidence levels, and
660: record-gate-soundness hypothesis;
661: Definition~\ref{def:record-gate-soundness-external-interface} isolates that
662: hypothesis as an external finite input/output interface, and
663: Proposition~\ref{prop:record-gate-soundness-external-interface-exactness} proves
664: that it has no hidden semantic or venue-acceptance content;
665: Definition~\ref{def:certificate-schema-acceptance-table} restates the finite
666: acceptance predicates as the table relation \(\mathsf{Acc}_{\Gamma}\);
667: Theorems~\ref{thm:verifier-input-output-contract},
668: \ref{thm:record-acceptance-exactness}, and
669: \ref{thm:finite-coordinate-determination-boundary} prove verifier exactness and
670: non-escalation from those predicates;
671: Theorem~\ref{thm:publication-safety-interface} constructs finite publication
672: certificates under record-gate soundness; and
673: Theorem~\ref{thm:current-package-certificate-exactness} specializes the calculus
674: to the current six-row package.  The later results about packaging, upload
675: conditions, digests, command runs, inventories, venue records, and case snapshots
676: are deliberately theoremized audit-record consequences of finite rows and tables.
677: They document the support surface and its negative boundary; they should not be
678: read as independent mathematical invariants or as substitutes for the printed
679: TeX proofs in the core chain.
680: 
681: \subsection{Abstract finite-record interface}
682: 
683: For this long technical supplement and its two-page CICM presentation artifact,
684: the following finite interface is the self-contained formal block used by every
685: later safety theorem.  It fixes, before use, the certificate tuple
686: \(\mathcal C(c)=(K,\ell,E,H,R,D,B)\), the dispatch table \(\Gamma\), the finite
687: verifier predicate \(V\), the acceptance relation \(\mathsf{Acc}_{\Gamma}\), the
688: record-gate-soundness predicate \(\mathsf{RGS}_{S}(P,L,\Gamma)\), and the
689: stronger-reading preorder \(\preceq_{\Gamma}\).  All hypotheses in the headline
690: safety theorem are finite-record hypotheses over these objects; none is supplied
691: by a hidden semantic oracle, by the manual theorem inventory, or by generated
692: prose.
693: 
694: \begin{definition}[Operational acceptance interface]
695: \label{def:operational-acceptance-interface}
696: A \emph{claim} is a manuscript assertion that can affect a mathematical,
697: artifact, route, or submission decision.  A \emph{load-bearing claim} is a claim
698: whose removal would change a theorem statement, a case-study conclusion, or the
699: submission-readiness status.  An \emph{advisory output} is any AI-agent draft,
700: score, suggested theorem, review packet, or repair proposal before a gate records
701: it.  A \emph{deterministic gate} is a repeatable predicate, script, or checklist
702: whose inputs and pass/fail decision are recorded independently of the prose
703: generated by an agent.  A \emph{human promotion} is the explicit editorial act
704: that moves a candidate into publication state, for example by creating an active
705: \texttt{2026\_*} paper directory with \texttt{main.tex} and \texttt{PIPELINE.md}.
706: An \emph{evidence surface} is the set of source paths, commits, commands, logs,
707: inventories, and witness records cited for a claim.  An \emph{acceptance entry}
708: is a manuscript or submission-ledger record that marks a candidate claim as
709: usable in the publication-facing argument.  \emph{Accepted work} is the subset of
710: candidate claims with such an entry.  This is a status notion only: the
711: definition records that an editorial acceptance entry exists, but it does not by
712: itself assert external editorial acceptance or implementation correctness.  A
713: pipeline is \emph{record-gate-sound for acceptance} when each acceptance entry records
714: the claim kind, the evidence surface, the evidence level, and a pass record for
715: every gate in $\Gamma$ assigned to that claim kind, and when a route-boundary
716: entry also records human promotion.
717: 
718: The deterministic dispatch function $\Gamma$ from claim kinds to gates is part
719: of the operational interface.  Its domain is
720: \[
721: \{\text{formal-source},\text{ traceability},\text{ route},
722: \text{ submission},\text{ artifact},\text{ discovery},\text{ work-selection}\}.
723: \]
724: It sends formal-source claims to the build and axiom-purity gates; traceability
725: claims to inventory and marker-existence gates; route claims to promotion,
726: overlap, and submission-state gates; submission claims to submission-pack gates;
727: artifact claims to status and limitation-ledger gates; discovery claims to the
728: pinned discovery gate above and, for extension claims, to the theorem-content
729: delta gate; and work-selection claims to the scheduler gate, whose evidence
730: surface is a critical-path score or backlog window together with the claim kind
731: that the selected item is meant to advance.  For mixed claims, $\Gamma$ returns
732: the union of the gate sets for the claim kinds present.
733: 
734: The route gates have separate operational meanings.  The \emph{promotion gate}
735: uses the active-paper evidence surface, namely a human promotion act producing a
736: \path{2026_*} directory with \path{main.tex} and \path{PIPELINE.md}; passing
737: means the candidate may be daemon-visible publication work, and failing means it
738: remains intake or source material.  The \emph{overlap gate} uses route identity,
739: semantic-overlap ledgers, submitted-sibling paths, and program-board state;
740: passing means advancement is route-disjoint or has a recorded resolution, and
741: failing means advancement is blocked until closure, merge, supersession, or
742: waiting for prior feedback.  The \emph{submission-state gate} uses submission
743: markers, venue state, board state, and submission-pack metadata; passing means
744: the route's publication state permits the proposed action, and failing means the
745: action would duplicate, race, or under-specify an active or submitted route.  The
746: \emph{theorem-content delta gate} uses theorem inventories, hard-lint output, and
747: delta records such as \path{ARTIFACT_INVENTORY.md}; passing means that an
748: extension claim identifies load-bearing mathematical content, and failing means
749: that compilation, file churn, or surface edits may not be reported as substantive
750: theorem growth.
751: 
752: A \emph{reviewable artifact} for this paper is either a public repository file,
753: a supplemental file included with the submission, or an explicit
754: historical-evidence boundary stating that the local path is used only as
755: path-verified evidence and not as a fresh command-run claim.  A \emph{case
756: witness record} is path-complete when its evidence surface gives a
757: concrete source or artifact path for each load-bearing component of the row.  A
758: route-overlap witness has the fields \emph{route identity}, \emph{submitted
759: sibling}, \emph{overlap reason}, \emph{gate decision}, and \emph{allowed next
760: actions}.  An artifact-limitation witness has the fields \emph{artifact status
761: path}, \emph{limitation ledger path}, \emph{observed limitation}, \emph{gate
762: decision}, and \emph{allowed manuscript claim}.  A promotion witness has the
763: fields \emph{candidate surface}, \emph{human promotion evidence},
764: \emph{active-paper markers}, \emph{gate decision}, and \emph{allowed manuscript
765: claim}.  A theorem-content witness has the fields \emph{changed surface},
766: \emph{theorem inventory or hard-lint surface}, \emph{substantive-content delta},
767: \emph{gate decision}, and \emph{allowed manuscript claim}.  The inventory files
768: \path{ARTIFACT_INVENTORY.md}, \path{SOURCE_MAP.md}, and
769: \path{review_bundle/REVIEW_BUNDLE_MANIFEST.json} are part of the evidence surface
770: for this paper and give the compact path catalogue used by
771: Table~\ref{tab:case-witnesses}.
772: 
773: We distinguish two evidence levels.  A claim is \emph{path-verified} when this
774: paper records the relevant source path, artifact path, pinned commit or local
775: publication path, and gate decision, but does not assert that a command was
776: freshly rerun for this submission.  A claim is \emph{command-run} when the paper
777: also records the command, source commit, environment, exit code, and log path
778: for a fresh run.  This long technical supplement uses path-verified architecture
779: and case evidence unless explicitly marked command-run.
780: 
781: A uniform \emph{pass record} is the finite tuple
782: \[
783: \rho=(g,I,d,e,\ell),
784: \]
785: where $g$ is the gate name, $I$ is the input surface submitted to that gate,
786: $d$ is the recorded decision, $e$ is the evidence pointer supporting the
787: decision, and $\ell$ is the evidence level.  A pass record is assigned to a
788: claim certificate only when its evidence pointer is contained in the claim's
789: evidence surface, in a submitted manifest or table row, in a bundled snapshot, or
790: in an explicit historical-evidence boundary.
791: 
792: A \emph{publication certificate} for a load-bearing claim $c$ is the finite
793: record
794: \[
795: \mathcal C(c)=(K,\ell,E,H,R,D,B).
796: \]
797: Here $K$ is the nonempty finite set of claim kinds carried by $c$, $\ell$ is the
798: evidence level, $E$ is the evidence surface, $H$ is either a human-promotion
799: record or the marker that $c$ does not cross the intake-to-publication boundary,
800: $R$ gives one pass record, with the five fields above, for each gate in
801: $\bigcup_{k\in K}\Gamma(k)$, $D$ is present exactly when $K$ contains discovery
802: and then names the pinned discovery-gate source-interface rows used for the
803: claim, and $B$ records either the command-run fields or the explicit boundary
804: that no command-run assertion is made.  A certificate is \emph{locally
805: checkable} when these finite fields are present and every path, manifest row, or
806: log named by a field lies in the submitted artifact, the supplemental review
807: bundle, or the explicit path-verified historical boundary.
808: 
809: For later use we make the local check explicit as a finite verifier.  The
810: concrete package checker supplied with the review bundle is
811: \path{review_bundle/verify_certificate_records.py}.  Its positive invocation is
812: \path{python review_bundle/verify_certificate_records.py}; it reads exactly
813: \path{review_bundle/certificate_schema.json},
814: \path{review_bundle/current_package_pass_records.json},
815: \path{review_bundle/submission_interface_map.json},
816: \path{review_bundle/primary_claim_inventory.json}, and
817: \path{review_bundle/REVIEW_BUNDLE_MANIFEST.json}, and writes the recorded pass log
818: \path{review_bundle/certificate_verification_run.log}.  Its negative-example
819: invocation is
820: \path{python review_bundle/verify_certificate_records.py --negative-example review_bundle/negative_certificate_record_example.json},
821: with recorded log \path{review_bundle/negative_certificate_record_example_run.md};
822: that fixture upgrades a route row to command-run strength while omitting the
823: command-run tuple and is rejected with the finite bad subrecord
824: \path{neg1: command-run fields incomplete}.  The mathematical verifier below is
825: the schema abstraction of this package checker.
826: 
827: Let \(\mathsf{Dom}(\Gamma)\) be the seven-kind domain and let
828: \(\mathsf{Lev}=\{\mathsf{path},\mathsf{command}\}\).  For a candidate record
829: \(\mathcal C(c)=(K,\ell,E,H,R,D,B)\), define six finite predicates:
830: \begin{enumerate}
831: \item[$P_1$:] $K\ne\emptyset$, $K\subseteq\mathsf{Dom}(\Gamma)$, and
832: $\ell\in\mathsf{Lev}$.
833: \item[$P_2$:] $E$ is finite and each item of $E$ is a submitted path,
834: review-bundle path, manifest row, log path, pinned commit, or explicit
835: historical-evidence boundary.
836: \item[$P_3$:] for every gate $g\in\bigcup_{k\in K}\Gamma(k)$, $R$ contains a
837: pass record $(g,I,d,e,\ell')$ whose evidence pointer lies in $E$ or in a named
838: submitted table, manifest row, bundled snapshot, or historical boundary.
839: \item[$P_4$:] $H$ records the human promotion exactly when $c$ crosses the
840: intake-to-publication boundary and otherwise records that no such boundary is
841: crossed.
842: \item[$P_5$:] $D$ is present exactly when discovery lies in $K$, and then names
843: the pinned discovery-gate source-interface rows used by the claim.
844: \item[$P_6$:] $B$ either records command, source commit, environment, exit code,
845: and log path for command-run evidence or explicitly records that no command-run
846: assertion is made.
847: \end{enumerate}
848: Define the finite verifier
849: \[
850: V(c,\mathcal C)=\bigwedge_{i=1}^{6}P_i(c,\mathcal C,\Gamma).
851: \]
852: If this conjunction is false, the rejected subrecord is the finite set of fields
853: read by the least-indexed failed predicate.  This paragraph defines the verifier
854: only; no unprinted semantic oracle is part of it.  The next definition packages
855: the same finite fields as an explicit input type and a finite acceptance table;
856: the exactness theorem below then proves that the verifier and the independently
857: named record-acceptance relation coincide on that finite data structure.
858: 
859: For a package $P$ with ledger $L$, dispatch table $\Gamma$, and submitted
860: support surface $S$, write
861: \[
862: \mathsf{RGS}_{S}(P,L,\Gamma)
863: \]
864: for the following finite predicate.  For every acceptance entry of a
865: load-bearing claim $c$ in $L$, there is a publication certificate
866: $\mathcal C(c)=(K,\ell,E,H,R,D,B)$ such that $E\subseteq S$, $V(c,\mathcal C)=1$,
867: each pass record in $R$ is a submitted row of $L$, the row decision is passing,
868: and the row's evidence pointer denotes an element of $S$ at the evidence level
869: recorded in the row.  If $B$ does not contain command, source state,
870: environment, exit code, and log path, then $\mathsf{RGS}_{S}$ asserts only this
871: finite row-and-pointer property and asserts no implementation-level correctness
872: of the gate that produced the row.
873: \end{definition}
874: 
875: \begin{definition}[Record-gate soundness external interface]
876: \label{def:record-gate-soundness-external-interface}
877: The external record-gate interface for a package is the finite predicate
878: \[
879: \mathsf{RGS}_{S}(P,L,\Gamma)
880: \]
881: with the following inputs and outputs.  Its inputs are exactly a package $P$, a
882: submitted support surface $S$, a finite acceptance ledger $L$, and the deterministic
883: claim-kind dispatch table $\Gamma$ of
884: Definition~\ref{def:operational-acceptance-interface}.  Its positive output is the
885: finite family of certificates
886: \[
887: \{\mathcal C(c)=(K,\ell,E,H,R,D,B): c\in L
888: \text{ is an accepted load-bearing claim}\}
889: \]
890: such that $E\subseteq S$, $V(c,\mathcal C(c))=1$, every assigned gate in
891: $\bigcup_{k\in K}\Gamma(k)$ has a submitted passing row in $R$, each evidence
892: pointer in such a row denotes an element of $S$ at the recorded evidence level,
893: the human-promotion coordinate $H$ is present exactly at an intake-to-publication
894: crossing, and the discovery and command-boundary coordinates $D$ and $B$ have the
895: presence conditions required by the verifier predicates $P_5$ and $P_6$.  Its
896: negative output, when the predicate fails for a ledger row, is only the finite bad
897: subrecord read by the first failed verifier predicate among $P_1,\ldots,P_6$.
898: 
899: This interface makes no further claim.  In particular it does not assert that an
900: external venue accepted the work, that a command was freshly rerun, that a Lean
901: development builds, that an artifact has the semantic property named in prose, or
902: that an advisory agent output is proof evidence, unless the corresponding source
903: path, command, source state, environment, exit code, log path, or gate row is one
904: of the finite coordinates read above.
905: \end{definition}
906: 
907: \begin{proposition}[Record-gate interface equivalence and non-claim boundary]
908: \label{prop:record-gate-soundness-external-interface-exactness}
909: For the operational acceptance interface of
910: Definition~\ref{def:operational-acceptance-interface}, the standalone interface in
911: Definition~\ref{def:record-gate-soundness-external-interface} is equivalent to the
912: predicate $\mathsf{RGS}_{S}(P,L,\Gamma)$ used in the publication-safety theorem.
913: Moreover, under this interface every consequence used by
914: Theorem~\ref{thm:publication-safety-interface} is determined by the finite
915: coordinates $(P,S,L,\Gamma)$ and by the displayed certificates
916: $\mathcal C(c)$; no implementation-level, semantic, venue-acceptance,
917: fresh-execution, or advisory-evidence claim follows from record-gate soundness
918: unless it appears as one of those finite coordinates.
919: \end{proposition}
920: 
921: \begin{proof}
922: The predicate $\mathsf{RGS}_{S}(P,L,\Gamma)$ in
923: Definition~\ref{def:operational-acceptance-interface} quantifies over exactly the
924: accepted load-bearing ledger entries $c\in L$ and requires exactly the following
925: data: a certificate $\mathcal C(c)=(K,\ell,E,H,R,D,B)$, containment $E\subseteq S$,
926: acceptance by the finite verifier $V$, submitted passing rows for the pass records
927: in $R$, and evidence pointers denoting elements of $S$ at the recorded evidence
928: levels.  The verifier definition expands $V(c,\mathcal C(c))=1$ into the six
929: predicates $P_1,\ldots,P_6$, which are precisely the kind, evidence-surface,
930: assigned-gate, human-promotion, discovery, and command-boundary presence
931: conditions listed in
932: Definition~\ref{def:record-gate-soundness-external-interface}.  Hence a positive
933: instance of one definition supplies a positive instance of the other, with the
934: same certificates and the same submitted rows; conversely the standalone
935: interface supplies every finite field required by the original predicate, so the
936: original predicate holds.
937: 
938: For the boundary statement, inspect the fields read by $P_1,\ldots,P_6$.  They
939: contain claim kinds, evidence levels, finite evidence pointers, human-promotion
940: markers, assigned gate rows, discovery rows, and the command-run or no-command-run
941: boundary.  None of these fields is a venue decision, a proof-assistant semantic
942: judgment, an artifact semantic evaluator, a live daemon state, or an advisory
943: agent proof object unless an additional row explicitly records that fact as an
944: evidence coordinate satisfying the same verifier predicates.  Therefore the
945: interface can be used in Theorem~\ref{thm:publication-safety-interface} only to
946: construct finite publication certificates and finite bad subrecords.  Any stronger
947: reading would require an added coordinate and is outside the predicate just proved
948: equivalent.
949: \end{proof}
950: 
951: \begin{definition}[Stronger-reading preorder]
952: \label{def:stronger-reading-preorder}
953: Fix the dispatch table $\Gamma$ and certificate tuple
954: $\mathcal C(c)=(K,\ell,E,H,R,D,B)$ of
955: Definition~\ref{def:operational-acceptance-interface}.  A proposed reading
956: $c^{+}$ of the same prose claim is \emph{record-stronger} than $c$, written
957: $c\preceq_{\Gamma} c^{+}$, if at least one of the following finite coordinates is
958: strictly enlarged while the others are unchanged or enlarged: the claim-kind set
959: $K$; the evidence level, from $\mathsf{path}$ to $\mathsf{command}$; the evidence
960: surface $E$; the promotion field $H$; the assigned gate-pass family $R$; the
961: discovery-interface field $D$; or the boundary field $B$.  It is
962: \emph{properly stronger} if the enlargement is strict in at least one coordinate.
963: It is \emph{accepted as a stronger reading} only when the enlarged tuple still
964: satisfies $\mathsf{Acc}_{\Gamma}$: every newly added kind has its assigned gates,
965: every newly raised evidence level has the command tuple required by A6, and every
966: new evidence item is on the submitted support surface or in an explicit
967: historical boundary.
968: 
969: The preorder is intentionally syntactic and finite-record based.  It is not a
970: semantic entailment relation between mathematical propositions, and it does not
971: turn an inventory entry, digest row, or path reference into proof evidence for a
972: theorem-like environment.
973: \end{definition}
974: 
975: \begin{definition}[Certificate-schema acceptance table]
976: \label{def:certificate-schema-acceptance-table}
977: Fix the seven-kind dispatch table $\Gamma$ of
978: Definition~\ref{def:operational-acceptance-interface}.  The verifier input for a
979: load-bearing claim $c$ is the following finite record type:
980: \[
981: \mathcal C(c)=
982: \{K,\ell,E,H,R,D,B\}.
983: \]
984: Its typed fields are these and only these: $K$ is a finite set of claim kinds;
985: $\ell\in\{\mathsf{path},\mathsf{command}\}$ is the declared evidence level; $E$
986: is a finite evidence-surface list; $H$ is the route-boundary field; $R$ is a
987: finite family of gate pass records, each with gate name, input surface, decision,
988: evidence pointer, and evidence level; $D$ is either empty or a finite list of
989: pinned discovery source-interface rows; and $B$ is the command-boundary field.
990: This is a data schema, not a theorem: all later exactness claims must be proved
991: from this displayed record and cannot appeal to unlisted package state.  Define
992: $\mathsf{Acc}_{\Gamma}(c,\mathcal C)$ to be the conjunction of the six finite
993: table predicates A1--A6 displayed below; no clause may query generated
994: prose, a live daemon, an artifact evaluator, a proof assistant, or a venue state
995: outside these fields and their cited submitted surfaces.
996: 
997: \begin{center}
998: \centering
999: \small
1000: \begin{tabularx}{\linewidth}{p{0.08\linewidth}p{0.22\linewidth}X}
1001: \toprule
1002: Clause & Field read & Acceptance condition \\
1003: \midrule
1004: A1 & $K,\ell$ & $K$ is a nonempty subset of the seven-kind domain and $\ell$ is one of the two evidence levels. \\
1005: A2 & $E$ & Every evidence item is a submitted path, review-bundle path, manifest row, log path, pinned commit, or explicit historical-evidence boundary available at level $\ell$. \\
1006: A3 & $K,R,E$ & For every gate in $\bigcup_{k\in K}\Gamma(k)$, $R$ has a pass record with gate name, input surface, decision, evidence pointer, and evidence level; the pointer lies in $E$ or in a named submitted table, manifest row, bundled snapshot, or historical boundary. \\
1007: A4 & $H$ & $H$ records human promotion exactly when $c$ crosses the intake-to-publication boundary, and otherwise records that no such crossing occurs. \\
1008: A5 & $K,D$ & $D$ is present exactly when discovery lies in $K$, and then names the pinned discovery-gate source-interface rows used by $c$. \\
1009: A6 & $\ell,B$ & $B$ records command, source commit, environment, exit code, and log path for command-run evidence, or records that no command-run assertion is made. \\
1010: \bottomrule
1011: \end{tabularx}
1012: \end{center}
1013: \end{definition}
1014: 
1015: \begin{proposition}[Finite certificate schema verifier as a record check]
1016: \label{prop:finite-certificate-schema-verifier}
1017: For every load-bearing manuscript claim $c$ whose component kinds lie in the
1018: seven-kind domain, $V(c,\mathcal C)$ is a finite, prose-insensitive
1019: record-schema verifier for the declared publication certificate.  The submitted
1020: package instantiates $V$ by the script
1021: \path{review_bundle/verify_certificate_records.py}.  On the positive package
1022: input it reads the schema file, pass-record ledger, submission-interface map,
1023: primary-claim inventory, and review-bundle manifest named above, and outputs
1024: acceptance in \path{review_bundle/certificate_verification_run.log}.  On the
1025: negative fixture \path{review_bundle/negative_certificate_record_example.json} it
1026: outputs rejection in \path{review_bundle/negative_certificate_record_example_run.md}
1027: with the bad subrecord \path{neg1: command-run fields incomplete}.  If $V$
1028: accepts, then $\mathcal C(c)$ is locally checkable at exactly its declared
1029: evidence level.  This is certificate-record verification only: it checks finite
1030: record shape, dispatch, evidence pointers, and command-boundary discipline, not
1031: end-to-end external semantic validation of the referenced artifacts or source
1032: theory.  If $V$ rejects, then a reviewer can extract a finite bad subrecord
1033: consisting of the first failed schema check and the fields on which that check
1034: depends.
1035: \end{proposition}
1036: 
1037: \begin{proof}
1038: The verifier inspects only the displayed tuple $\mathcal C(c)$, the finite
1039: seven-kind domain, the finite dispatch table $\Gamma$, and the finite list of
1040: paths, rows, commits, or logs cited by the record.  The package script realizes
1041: these inputs as five finite JSON files: the schema supplies the seven-kind
1042: domain, evidence levels, gate dispatch table, required record fields, required
1043: pass-record fields, accepted decisions, manifest-required files, and accepted
1044: artifact-prefix patterns; the ledger supplies the seven current records $a_1,
1045: \ldots,a_7$; the submission-interface map and primary-claim inventory supply the
1046: finite manuscript-facing row sets; and the manifest supplies the submitted bundle
1047: paths.  The script output is either the pass transcript in
1048: \path{certificate_verification_run.log} or a list of \path{ERROR: ...} lines,
1049: each naming the failed finite condition.  Since $K$ is required to be a subset of
1050: the seven-kind domain, the set \(\bigcup_{k\in K}\Gamma(k)\) is finite.  The
1051: checks over $E$, $R$, and the command-run fields are therefore finite lookups in
1052: named manuscript or bundle surfaces, not semantic judgments about generated
1053: prose.
1054: 
1055: If all six checks pass, the tuple has exactly the fields required in the
1056: definition of a publication certificate: a finite kind set $K$, declared evidence
1057: level $\ell$, evidence surface $E$, route-boundary field $H$, gate-record field
1058: $R$, optional discovery field $D$ with the required presence condition, and
1059: evidence-strength boundary $B$.  Since $V$ is the conjunction of the explicit
1060: predicates $P_1,\ldots,P_6$, the six table clauses A1--A6 hold for exactly the
1061: same finite inputs.  Check~(ii) is precisely the local-checkability condition on
1062: cited paths, manifest rows, logs, pinned commits, and historical boundaries.
1063: Checks~(iii)--(vi) prevent record-level escalations of the declared level:
1064: missing gate-passage records, unrecorded route promotion, unsupported discovery
1065: routing, and command-run assertion without command-run fields.  Thus an accepted
1066: record is locally checkable at the level $\ell$ it declares, and no stronger
1067: level is silently inferred by the record checker.
1068: 
1069: If a check fails, the verifier stops at a finite test.  The bad subrecord is the
1070: finite collection of fields read by that failed test: for example, $K$ and
1071: $\Gamma$ for a gate-domain failure, $E$ for an evidence-surface failure, $K$,
1072: $\Gamma$, $R$, and the cited evidence item for a gate-record failure, $H$ for a
1073: promotion failure, $K$ and $D$ for a discovery-field failure, or $\ell$ and $B$
1074: for an evidence-level failure.  The supplied negative fixture is exactly the last
1075: case: it declares $\ell=\mathsf{command}$ but provides no
1076: \path{boundary.command_run_fields} containing command, source commit,
1077: environment, exit code, and log path, so the bad subrecord is the pair consisting
1078: of the evidence-level field and the boundary field.  This subrecord is
1079: independent of any surrounding paragraph that may have described the claim.\end{proof}
1080: 
1081: \begin{theorem}[Verifier input--predicate--output contract]
1082: \label{thm:verifier-input-output-contract}
1083: The verifier interface used by the publication-safety theorem is exactly the
1084: following finite mathematical-software contract.  Its input is a pair
1085: $(c,\mathcal C(c))$, where $c$ is a load-bearing manuscript claim and
1086: \(\mathcal C(c)=(K,\ell,E,H,R,D,B)\) is the certificate record of
1087: Definition~\ref{def:certificate-schema-acceptance-table}, together with the fixed
1088: dispatch table $\Gamma$ and the submitted support surface $S$.  Its checked
1089: predicates and outputs are:
1090: \begin{center}
1091: \centering
1092: \small
1093: \begin{tabularx}{\linewidth}{p{0.12\linewidth}p{0.30\linewidth}X}
1094: \toprule
1095: Check & Predicate actually checked & Possible output \\
1096: \midrule
1097: $Q_1$ & $K$ is nonempty, $K\subseteq\mathsf{Dom}(\Gamma)$, and $\ell\in\{\mathsf{path},\mathsf{command}\}$ & accept this coordinate, or reject with bad fields $K,\ell$ \\
1098: $Q_2$ & each item of $E$ is on $S$ or in an explicitly declared pinned, logged, or historical boundary & accept this coordinate, or reject with bad field $E$ and the offending pointer \\
1099: $Q_3$ & every gate assigned by $\Gamma$ to a kind in $K$ has a passing five-field record in $R$ whose pointer is reviewable through $E$, a submitted table, a manifest row, a bundled snapshot, or an historical boundary & accept this coordinate, or reject with bad fields $K,R,E$ and the missing or non-reviewable gate \\
1100: $Q_4$ & $H$ records exactly the human-promotion crossing asserted by $c$, or records that no such crossing is asserted & accept this coordinate, or reject with bad field $H$ \\
1101: $Q_5$ & $D$ is present exactly for discovery-kind claims and then names only pinned source-interface rows used by $c$ & accept this coordinate, or reject with bad fields $K,D$ \\
1102: $Q_6$ & $B$ either contains command, source state, environment, exit code, and log path, or explicitly marks that no command-run assertion is made & accept this coordinate, or reject with bad fields $\ell,B$ \\
1103: \bottomrule
1104: \end{tabularx}
1105: \end{center}
1106: The positive output is only the finite acceptance bit
1107: $\mathsf{accept}(c,\mathcal C)$ for these six predicates.  The negative output is
1108: only the finite bad subrecord belonging to the first failed predicate.  In
1109: particular, the contract has no output channel for proof-assistant validity,
1110: daemon correctness, artifact semantics, fresh case-study reruns, venue upload, or
1111: venue acceptance.  Therefore any theorem in this paper that invokes the verifier
1112: can certify exactly finite record acceptance for the submitted schema and cannot
1113: certify end-to-end workflow correctness unless an enlarged input schema and an
1114: additional proved verifier contract are supplied.
1115: \end{theorem}
1116: 
1117: \begin{proof}
1118: The input type is displayed in
1119: Definition~\ref{def:certificate-schema-acceptance-table}: after the fixed table
1120: $\Gamma$ is chosen, the only variable fields are $K,\ell,E,H,R,D,B$.  The
1121: submitted support surface $S$ is used only to decide whether the finite pointers
1122: named in those fields are reviewer-visible at the declared level.  Thus every
1123: listed predicate is a finite Boolean predicate on the displayed input fields and
1124: on the fixed submitted support surface.
1125: 
1126: For each row, the predicate is identical to one of the six predicates used in the
1127: definition of $V$: $Q_i=P_i$ for $1\leq i\leq 6$, with the same fields read and
1128: with the same failure subrecord, namely the fields read by the least-indexed
1129: failed predicate.  Proposition~\ref{prop:finite-certificate-schema-verifier}
1130: proves that the bundled Python checker is an implementation witness for this
1131: finite schema: it reads the schema, pass-record ledger, submission-interface map,
1132: primary-claim inventory, and manifest, and returns either the pass transcript or
1133: finite error records.  The theorem here is the abstract verifier contract behind
1134: that implementation witness, so its output alphabet is precisely
1135: \(\{\mathsf{accept}\}\) together with the finite bad-subrecord reports.
1136: 
1137: The final boundary follows by inspection of the input and output alphabets.  No
1138: field in $\mathcal C(c)$ contains an object-language proof derivation, a theorem
1139: prover kernel trace, a daemon transition semantics, an artifact evaluator
1140: semantics, a fresh rerun transcript for the historical case studies, or a venue
1141: receipt.  No output of the contract returns such an object either.  A conclusion
1142: depending on one of those objects is therefore not a function of the declared
1143: input record.  It can become checkable only after the schema is enlarged to add
1144: that object as an input coordinate and after a new verifier contract for the
1145: enlarged coordinate is proved.  Hence the present verifier certifies only finite
1146: record acceptance and its finite rejection witnesses.\end{proof}
1147: 
1148: \begin{theorem}[Schema-relative finite-record acceptance exactness]
1149: \label{thm:record-acceptance-exactness}
1150: Fix the seven-kind dispatch table $\Gamma$, the two evidence levels, and the
1151: typed certificate tuple
1152: $\mathcal C(c)=(K,\ell,E,H,R,D,B)$ of
1153: Definition~\ref{def:certificate-schema-acceptance-table}.  For every
1154: load-bearing claim $c$ and every finite candidate certificate $\mathcal C(c)$,
1155: the finite verifier $V$ of
1156: Definition~\ref{def:operational-acceptance-interface} and
1157: Theorem~\ref{thm:verifier-input-output-contract} accepts $\mathcal C(c)$ if
1158: and only if the six-clause table predicate
1159: $\mathsf{Acc}_{\Gamma}(c,\mathcal C)$ holds.  This is a schema-relative and
1160: finite-record-relative assertion: both sides read exactly the fields named in the
1161: fixed schema and in the finite submitted record surface, and neither side ranges
1162: over unrecorded semantic facts about the referenced mathematics, artifacts, or
1163: gate implementations.  Moreover, if $V$ accepts a record for $c$, the same record
1164: cannot certify any stronger reading $c^{+}$ that adds a new claim kind, a stronger
1165: evidence level, an omitted route crossing, an omitted discovery interface, an
1166: omitted pass record, or a command-run assertion: such a reading has a finite bad
1167: subrecord of the corresponding clause.  Thus $V$ is exact for this paper's finite
1168: record-acceptance relation over the submitted source, tables, manifests, logs,
1169: and explicitly declared historical boundaries, while remaining silent about
1170: external semantic validity of the referenced theory or artifacts and about the
1171: implementation-level soundness of any deployed gate implementation beyond the
1172: recorded pass rows.
1173: \end{theorem}
1174: 
1175: \begin{proof}
1176: The definition of $V$ and the six clauses above use the same finite inputs.
1177: Predicate $P_1$ is clause~A1, $P_2$ is clause~A2, and $P_3$ is clause~A3,
1178: including both assigned-gate coverage and the requirement that each evidence
1179: pointer be tied to the declared review surface.  Predicates $P_4$, $P_5$, and
1180: $P_6$ are exactly clauses~A4, A5, and A6.  Therefore, if
1181: $\mathsf{Acc}_{\Gamma}(c,\mathcal C)$ holds, every verifier predicate is true
1182: and $V$ accepts.
1183: 
1184: Conversely, suppose $V$ accepts.  Reading the successful checks in order gives
1185: precisely clauses~1--6: the kind set and evidence level are in the permitted
1186: domains; the evidence surface is reviewer-available at the declared level; every
1187: gate assigned by $\Gamma$ has a pass record with the required five fields and an
1188: allowed pointer; promotion and non-promotion are separated by $H$; discovery-kind
1189: claims and only discovery-kind claims carry $D$; and $B$ fixes the command-run
1190: boundary.  Hence $\mathsf{Acc}_{\Gamma}(c,\mathcal C)$ holds.
1191: 
1192: For the final assertion, keep the accepted tuple fixed and consider a proposed
1193: stronger reading $c^{+}$.  If $c^{+}$ adds a claim kind, the finite set of gates
1194: assigned by $\Gamma$ grows unless the added kind is vacuous in this interface;
1195: without the corresponding pass records clause~3 fails, and if the kind lies
1196: outside the seven-kind domain clause~1 fails.  If $c^{+}$ raises the evidence
1197: level to command-run, clause~6 fails unless the command, commit, environment,
1198: exit code, and log path are present.  If it adds a route crossing, clause~4 fails
1199: unless $H$ contains the human-promotion record.  If it adds discovery content,
1200: clause~5 fails unless the pinned discovery rows are named.  If it asserts a new
1201: gate-dependent conclusion without a pass record, clause~3 fails.  In each case
1202: the failed clause reads only finitely many fields, so deleting irrelevant unread
1203: fields gives an inclusion-minimal bad subrecord.  If none of the clauses changes,
1204: then $c^{+}$ is not stronger in the record semantics: the certificate still
1205: licenses only the same kind set, same evidence level, same gate records, same
1206: route boundary, same discovery interface, and same command boundary.\end{proof}
1207: 
1208: \begin{corollary}[Executable schema exactness and negative fixture]
1209: \label{cor:executable-schema-exactness-negative-fixture}
1210: Under the hypotheses and fixed schema of
1211: Theorem~\ref{thm:record-acceptance-exactness}, the executable verifier instance
1212: \path{review_bundle/verify_certificate_records.py} is a finite witness of the
1213: same schema-relative relation, not an additional semantic oracle.  On the positive
1214: submitted package it can witness exactly the records satisfying
1215: \(\mathsf{Acc}_{\Gamma}\) at their declared evidence levels.  On the negative
1216: fixture
1217: \path{review_bundle/negative_certificate_record_example.json}, which upgrades a
1218: route record to command-run strength while omitting the command, source state,
1219: environment, exit code, and log path, the extracted bad subrecord is exactly the
1220: evidence-level declaration together with the incomplete boundary field.  Hence
1221: the executable example separates the verifier contract from any stronger reading:
1222: changing prose or ambient pipeline state while keeping the finite schema fields
1223: fixed cannot change either the acceptance bit or the first failed finite
1224: subrecord.
1225: \end{corollary}
1226: 
1227: \begin{proof}
1228: Proposition~\ref{prop:finite-certificate-schema-verifier} identifies the script
1229: \path{review_bundle/verify_certificate_records.py}, the schema files it reads,
1230: and the two recorded runs.  Its positive run returns the finite acceptance
1231: transcript for the submitted records, and its negative run returns the reported
1232: finite error \path{neg1: command-run fields incomplete}.  By
1233: Theorem~\ref{thm:verifier-input-output-contract}, those are the only two output
1234: kinds of the verifier contract: acceptance of the six predicates or a bad
1235: subrecord for the first failed predicate.  By
1236: Theorem~\ref{thm:record-acceptance-exactness}, positive acceptance is equivalent
1237: to \(\mathsf{Acc}_{\Gamma}\) for the same tuple
1238: \((K,\ell,E,H,R,D,B)\).  In the negative fixture, clauses A1--A4 and the
1239: non-discovery discipline are supplied for the route row, while the declared
1240: command-run level triggers clause A6 and the required five command-run fields are
1241: absent.  The first failed predicate therefore reads exactly \(\ell\) and \(B\),
1242: so deleting any unrelated prose, score, route history, or ambient implementation
1243: state leaves the same failure.  The script is thus an executable finite-record
1244: witness for the schema-relative theorem and for its negative certificate example,
1245: not evidence of an unrecorded semantic rerun or gate-implementation proof.\end{proof}
1246: 
1247: \begin{theorem}[Finite-coordinate determination and trust-boundary classifier]
1248: \label{thm:finite-coordinate-determination-boundary}
1249: Fix the dispatch table $\Gamma$ and the verifier $V$.  Let $F_{\Gamma}$ be the
1250: finite schema projection read by $V$ from a certificate record, namely the
1251: claim-kind set, declared evidence level, evidence surface, promotion field,
1252: gate-pass rows, discovery-source rows, command-boundary tuple, and the assigned
1253: gate set determined by $\Gamma$.  If two ambient pipeline or artifact states
1254: induce the same values of all coordinates in $F_{\Gamma}$ for a claim $c$, then
1255: they induce the same record-acceptance decision and the same finite publication
1256: certificate for $c$.  Therefore the exactness of
1257: Theorem~\ref{thm:record-acceptance-exactness} is exactness only relative to this
1258: finite schema projection.  No property depending on a coordinate outside
1259: $F_{\Gamma}$---for example the semantic truth of a referenced theorem, truthful
1260: production or implementation soundness of a gate row, a fresh Lean build result,
1261: a daemon trace, an artifact semantic fact, a theorem-environment completeness
1262: claim, or an external venue decision---is a consequence of the accepted
1263: certificate unless that property is first encoded as an additional finite
1264: certificate coordinate and the enlarged record is accepted.  In particular,
1265: record-gate soundness is a load-bearing hypothesis for
1266: Theorem~\ref{thm:publication-safety-interface}; it is not supplied by schema
1267: exactness alone.
1268: \end{theorem}
1269: 
1270: \begin{proof}
1271: The verifier $V$ is defined as an ordered list of six checks, and each check reads
1272: only one of the coordinates listed in $F_{\Gamma}$ together with the fixed finite
1273: dispatch table $\Gamma$.  If two ambient states induce the same values on
1274: $F_{\Gamma}$, then the input tuple \((K,\ell,E,H,R,D,B)\), the assigned gate set,
1275: and every submitted pointer queried by $V$ are identical in the two states.  Each
1276: of the six checks therefore has the same truth value in the two states, so $V$
1277: either accepts both records or rejects both records at the same first failed
1278: finite field.  By Theorem~\ref{thm:record-acceptance-exactness}, an accepted
1279: decision is equivalent to the same six-clause record-acceptance semantics, hence
1280: the finite certificate determined by those clauses is also the same.  This proves
1281: the schema-relative and finite-record-relative factorisation.
1282: 
1283: Now let $Q$ be a property whose truth can vary while all coordinates in
1284: $F_{\Gamma}$ are held fixed.  Then there exist two ambient states with the same
1285: certificate tuple and the same verifier decision but different truth values for
1286: $Q$.  A conclusion whose truth changes under such an $F_{\Gamma}$-preserving
1287: substitution is not determined by the accepted certificate.  To make $Q$
1288: load-bearing, the operational interface must add a finite coordinate recording
1289: the required evidence: for a build or daemon trace this is a command-run tuple;
1290: for an artifact semantic fact this is an artifact pass record or fresh evaluator
1291: rerun log; for theorem-environment completeness this is an extractor command,
1292: source state, environment, exit code, and log path; and for a venue decision this
1293: is an external evidence surface and the corresponding route/submission record.
1294: Without that enlargement, Theorem~\ref{thm:record-acceptance-exactness} supplies
1295: only the original finite-record conclusion.
1296: 
1297: Finally, the same argument isolates record-gate soundness.  The predicate
1298: $\mathsf{RGS}_{S}(P,L,\Gamma)$ says that recorded ledger entries actually contain
1299: the assigned passing rows and submitted evidence pointers.  A package can agree
1300: on the schema shape of a candidate certificate while differing on whether such a
1301: record-gate-sound ledger has been supplied.  Thus schema exactness checks the
1302: finite acceptance relation after the row data are present; it does not by itself
1303: make those rows available, truthfully produced, or semantically adequate.
1304: Theorem~\ref{thm:publication-safety-interface} therefore states record-gate
1305: soundness as an explicit hypothesis, and the trust boundary is exactly the finite
1306: projection $F_{\Gamma}$ plus that hypothesis.
1307: \end{proof}
1308: 